$PLUTOROOT/pluto/vec/vec.p:131:42: items.data is read here without holding mu, which guards it
    Every write to items.data that another task may run at the same time holds
    mu: pluto/vec/vec.p:428 in (*Vec).realloc (the task started by task grow at
    main.p:34). So the compiler infers that mu guards items.data, and every read
    must hold it too: a read without it may run while a write is half done.
    Here the main task reads it at pluto/vec/vec.p:131 in (*Vec).Ptr with no
    lock held.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Hold the lock while reading:
        mu.Lock()
        v := items.data
        mu.Unlock()
    (see "Data race freedom" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3503" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:25:10: items.data[i] is read here through a view that is not valid here: mu, which guards it, was not held from the load of the view to here
    Items.data[i] is read at main.p:25 in peek through a view loaded from shared
    memory. A view loaded from memory a lock guards stays valid only while a
    lock held at the load stays held: an Unlock, an RUnlock or a Wait of it, or
    a call that may release it, ends the view, even if the lock is taken again.
    Once the lock is released another task may change, move or free what the
    view points to.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Keep the lock held from the load of the view to its last use, or load the
    view again after taking the lock
    (see "Data race freedom" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3501" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:27:3: mu.Unlock() releases mu, which is not held here: it is not held on every path to here
    A lock is released where the code that releases it does not hold it. Every
    Unlock or RUnlock of a sync.Mutex, a sync.RWMutex or an rt.Lock, and every
    Wait(l) of a sync.Cond, must release a lock held on every path to the call,
    in write mode for Unlock and Wait and in read mode for RUnlock. A lock the
    compiler cannot name cannot be shown held: one reached through an interface
    such as sync.Locker, a func value or a method value, a pointer loaded from
    memory, or an array element with a variable index. A task, a scoped task and
    an interrupt handler start with no lock held, so none of them can release a
    lock that another party took.
    Release the lock in the function that took it, on every path, and name it
    the same way at both calls: copy a pointer into a local once (l :=
    &locks[i]) and use the local for both. To let another task end the work,
    send it a message on a channel instead of handing it a locked lock.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    (see "Data race freedom" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3504" for a longer explanation)
